<!--
  Junction page § Built for production. Facts: packages/junction/README.md
  (§ Idempotency-Key, Circuit breaker, CSRF protection, Health and metrics),
  src/core/idempotency.ts (the 24-hour ttl, the 409 and 422, the released key,
  no principal means no claim), src/core/hooks-resilience.ts (any throw counts,
  a failure in half-open reopens, state held in the hook's closure),
  src/transport/middleware.ts (the csrf order, allowMissingOrigin defaults true)
  and src/config/index.ts (idempotency enabled by default). The proof is a real
  run of a scratch app against packages/junction, kept in consts: a brace in
  markup opens a Mesa expression.
  Not claimed: replay across instances (the keys live in the app cache, one
  process), a replay marker header (the repeat carries none), and a breaker that
  tells a 4xx from an outage (it counts every throw).
-->
<script>
  const KEY = `$ curl -i -X POST localhost:8461/charges -H 'Authorization: Bearer …' \\
    -H 'Idempotency-Key: ord-7f3a' -d '{"amount":4200}'
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
{"id":"ch_1","amount":4200,"run":1}

# the same request again
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
{"id":"ch_1","amount":4200,"run":1}

# the same key, a different amount
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
{"name":"Unprocessable","message":"Idempotency key reused with a different
payload (charges.create). A key names one request; use a new one.", … "retryable":false}

# a new key
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
{"id":"ch_2","amount":4200,"run":2}

# the server's log for all four
[handler] charges.create ran (run #1)
[handler] charges.create ran (run #2)`

  const BREAKER = `$ curl localhost:8461/rates     # five times, upstream down, threshold 3
{"name":"GeneralError","message":"ECONNREFUSED: …","code":500,"data":null}     ×3
{"name":"Unavailable","message":"Circuit open for rates.find — too many recent failures","code":503,"data":null}
{"name":"Unavailable","message":"Circuit open for rates.find — …","code":503,"data":null}

# the server's log: three calls reached the upstream, not five
[handler] rates.find reached the upstream     ×3`
</script>

<section id="hardened" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Built for production</p>
    <h2>Safe to retry, quick to give up.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The parts an API usually gains after its first incident come in the package: a key that
      makes a retried write run once, a breaker for an upstream that is down, a CSRF check for
      cookie sessions and probes for the orchestrator.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>A retried write runs once.</strong>
          On by default, a mutating call carrying an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> from a signed-in
          caller runs once, and its repeat replays the first answer for 24 hours, from that
          process's cache. A duplicate still in flight is a retryable 409, the same key on a
          different body is a 422, and a call that failed frees its key.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A failing upstream fails fast.</strong>
          Add <code>circuitBreaker()</code> as an around hook and, after a run of consecutive
          failures (five by default), the service answers 503 without calling out for 30 seconds,
          then tries again, and one more failure reopens it. It counts every error the call throws.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CSRF checks where cookies make CSRF possible.</strong>
          Turning on cookie sessions installs <code>csrf()</code>, which refuses a write from another
          site or a sibling subdomain unless its origin is on your list.
          Bearer-token calls need no check, and a request with no origin information at all passes
          unless you set <code>allowMissingOrigin: false</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Probes an orchestrator can read.</strong>
          <code>health()</code> mounts <code>/health</code>, a 503 when a check you name fails or
          the app is draining, and <code>/health/live</code>, which consults no check. Outside
          development <code>/metrics</code> stays unmounted until you give it a token or an auth
          function, since it names the pid and every service.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <pre class="code">{KEY}</pre>
        <pre class="code">{BREAKER}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          A real run on Bun against <code>packages/junction</code>: a scratch app with a
          <code>charges</code> service whose <code>create</code> counts its own runs, and a
          <code>rates</code> service whose <code>find</code> calls an upstream that is not
          listening, under <code>circuitBreaker</code> with a threshold of 3. The caller signs in
          through the stub auth in <code>@frontierjs/junction/testing</code>. Abridged: the
          security headers on each answer and the repeated curl lines are cut, long bodies are
          shortened with <code>…</code>, and the <code>#</code> lines and <code>×3</code> are ours.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/README.md#idempotency-key">Idempotency-Key</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/README.md#circuit-breaker">circuit breaker</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/README.md#csrf-protection">CSRF protection</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/README.md#health-and-metrics">health and metrics</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/docs/hazards.md">what bites</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

